==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|wīt}}, {{IPA|/waɪt/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|aɪt|lang=en}}
* {{homophones|lang=en|wight}}; {{a|in accents with the [[wine-whine merger]]}} [[white]]

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|wīten||to accuse, reproach, punish, suspect}}, {{inh|en|ang|wītan||to look, behold, see, guard, keep, impute or ascribe to, accuse, reproach, blame}}, from  {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wītaną}}. Connected to {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|wīte}}, see below.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|wyte}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|wit}}

# {{label|en|chiefly|Scotland}} To [[blame]]; regard as [[guilty]], [[fault]], [[accuse]]
#* '''Late 14th century''', Geoffrey Chaucer, ‘The Wife of Bath's Tale’, ''Canterbury Tales'':
#*: As help me God, I shal þee nevere smyte! / Þat I have doon, it is þyself to '''wyte'''.
# To [[reproach]], [[censure]], [[mulct]]
# To [[observe]], [[keep]], [[guard]], [[preserve]], [[protect]]

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|wītan||guilt, blameworthiness, blame, wrongdoing, misdeed, offense, punishment, retribution, fine, bote, customary rent}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|wīte}}, see below.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{label|en|obsolete|outside|Scotland}} [[blame|Blame]], [[responsibility]], [[guilt]].
#* {{RQ:Mlry MArthrP1|I|XXVII|027}}:
#*: And so by fortune the ship drave unto a castle, and was all to-riven, and destroyed the most part{{nb...}}. So many lords and barons of this realm were displeased, for their children were so lost, and many put the '''wite''' on Merlin more than on Arthur; so what for dread and for love, they held their peace.
#*: <small>&bull; {{RQ:Mlry MrtDrthr|12.25|2|I|xxiij}}:
#*:: And so by fortune the shyp drofe vnto a castel and was al to ryuen and destroyed the most part{{nb...}}/ So many lordes and barons of this reame were displeasyd / for her children were so lost / and many put the '''wyte''' on Merlyn more than on Arthur / so what for drede and for loue they helde their pees</small>
#* {{quote-book|year=1922|author={{w|Eric Rücker Eddison|E. R. Eddison}}|title=[http://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks06/0602051.txt The Worm Ouroboros]
|publisher={{w|Project Gutenberg Australia|The Project Gutenberg, Australia}}
|passage=Nor I will not suffer mine indignation so to [[witwanton]] with fair justice as persuade me to put the '''wite''' on Witchland.}}
# [[punishment|Punishment]], [[penalty]], [[fine]], [[bote]], [[mulct]].

===Etymology 3===
From {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|witan}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|wit}}

# {{label|en|obsolete|or|poetic}} To [[go]], go away, [[depart]], [[perish]], [[vanish]]

